 # 11\. A polished 2-minute interview answer

 If they simply ask:

 **“Tell me about a major production incident you handled.”**

 I would use this version:

 Major Production Incident – XL-Release Storage Provisioning RCA

One major production incident I worked on involved an XL-Release deployment.

 The incident happened while the ALM team was implementing a change to enable application logs in XL-Release. The configuration change required us to reinstall XL-Release.

 After the reinstallation, the application pods did not come up. They were stuck in `Pending` state, and Kubernetes reported an error indicating that two PersistentVolumeClaims had unbound Persistent Volumes.

 I started troubleshooting from the application layer and followed the dependency chain. First, we confirmed that the pods were not starting because the PVCs were unbound. We then investigated why the Persistent Volumes were not being provisioned.

 Working together with the AWS team, we found that the underlying storage provisioning depended on connectivity between the EC2 instances and EFS. That connectivity was broken because a required VPC endpoint was missing.

 We traced the missing endpoint back to an earlier account cleanup activity. The endpoint had been considered unused and was deleted without sufficiently validating whether any active workload still depended on it.

 The immediate mitigation was to recreate the VPC endpoint in the CICD production account. Once the endpoint was recreated, EC2-to-EFS connectivity was restored, Persistent Volume provisioning succeeded, and we were able to complete the XL-Release reinstallation successfully.

 The root cause therefore had two aspects. The technical root cause was the missing VPC endpoint, which broke EC2-to-EFS connectivity. The underlying process issue was insufficient dependency and ownership validation during infrastructure cleanup.

 As a preventive measure, we established that resources must be verified with the relevant owning team before cleanup. We also recommended that housekeeping scripts be tested in a controlled environment, undergo quality checks, and receive architect or lead review before being used in production.

 The biggest lesson I took from this incident was not to stop troubleshooting at the first error message. The visible problem was a Kubernetes PVC issue, but the actual root cause was an AWS networking dependency. Following the dependency chain from application → Kubernetes → storage → AWS networking helped us identify the real root cause and restore the service quickly.

